# Section 1 of the British Nationality Act 1981, after the logic program of
# Sergot, Sadri, Kowalski, Kriwaczek, Hammond and Cory (1986). Anna, born in the
# United Kingdom after commencement to a settled father, is a British citizen,
# and so is her son Ben, through her. Hugo was born before commencement, Dara
# before her father settled, and Jo abroad; none of them is a citizen by
# section 1. The foundling Eve is a citizen; for Finn the contrary is shown.
# Shows "or" premises, absence as "unless the contrary is shown", date
# comparisons and citizenship that passes through a derived parent.

# Dates are integers of the form YYYYMMDD. The Act commenced on 1 January 1983.
19830101 is a commencement.

Farid is a parent of Anna and settledSince(Farid, 19750301).
Anna has birthPlace unitedKingdom and Anna has birthDate 19900512.
Anna is a parent of Ben.
Ben has birthPlace unitedKingdom and Ben has birthDate 20150820.
Anna is a parent of Jo.
Jo has birthPlace france and Jo has birthDate 20170203.

# Gwen is a citizen under earlier law, which this program does not model.
Gwen is a britishCitizen and Gwen is a parent of Hugo.
Hugo has birthPlace unitedKingdom and Hugo has birthDate 19800101.

Ivan is a parent of Dara and settledSince(Ivan, 20050101).
Dara has birthPlace unitedKingdom and Dara has birthDate 20010304.

Eve is a foundling and Eve has foundIn unitedKingdom and Eve has foundDate 20200601.
Finn is a foundling and Finn has foundIn unitedKingdom and Finn has foundDate 20180101.
Finn is a contraryShown.

# A parent qualifies who, at the time of the birth, is a British citizen or
# is settled in the United Kingdom. "and" binds more tightly than "or".
For every Q and P and D and S,
if Q is a parent of P and Q is a britishCitizen
or Q is a parent of P and P has birthDate D and settledSince(Q, S) and S is at most D
then Q is a qualifyingParent of P.

# Section 1(1) and 1(2): born in the United Kingdom after commencement to a
# qualifying parent, or found abandoned there as a new-born after
# commencement, unless the contrary is shown.
For every P and Q and D and C,
if P has birthPlace unitedKingdom and P has birthDate D and C is a commencement
and D is at least C and Q is a qualifyingParent of P
or P is a foundling and P has foundIn unitedKingdom and P has foundDate D
and C is a commencement and D is at least C and it is not known that P is a contraryShown
then P is a britishCitizen.

Find every P such that P is a britishCitizen.
Find every Q and P such that Q is a qualifyingParent of P.
